DAY 24 — SYMBOLIC & HARD LINKS
Commands Reference Sheet
Machine: Kali Linux | User: raven
==========================================

--- CREATING A SYMBOLIC LINK ---
ln -s /path/to/original /path/to/link      # create a symlink (shortcut/pointer)
ln -s original.txt mylink.txt               # example, same directory

--- CREATING A HARD LINK ---
ln original.txt hardlink.txt                # create a hard link (another name for same data)
                                             # NOTE: no -s flag for hard links

--- VIEWING LINKS ---
ls -la                          # symlinks show as: link -> target (with 'l' file type)
                                 # hard links look like normal files, but link count > 1

readlink mylink.txt              # show what a symlink points to (relative path)
readlink -f mylink.txt            # show the FULL absolute path it resolves to

--- CHECKING LINK COUNT (for hard links) ---
ls -la file.txt                   # 2nd column = number of hard links to this data
stat file.txt                      # detailed info, including "Links:" count

--- REMOVING A LINK (doesn't affect the original) ---
rm mylink.txt                       # removes just the symlink, original stays intact
unlink mylink.txt                    # alternative to rm, specifically for links

--- KEY BEHAVIOR DIFFERENCES ---
# Symlink:  breaks if original is deleted; can cross filesystems; can link to directories
# Hard link: survives original deletion (data stays until ALL links removed);
#            CANNOT cross filesystems; CANNOT link to directories

